# Paper flow (v10) — Visual token allocation does not beat spending the tokens, and here is why

> **What changed from v9.** v9 led with the method. Its own margin never cleared zero (+3.9pp
> [−0.3,+8.2] pooled in its best regime, n=515), while the negative and mechanistic results are
> significant at n=800. v10 **leads with what the evidence supports** and keeps the method as the
> constructive §6, with its boundary stated. v9's scale-boundary mechanism is also **retracted**: a
> controlled resolution ablation refuted it, and the coverage account absorbs it (§7).
>
> This is **not** a protocol-only paper. §6 is a real method with a measured win in a characterised
> regime; it is simply not the claim the statistics can carry as a headline.

---

## The claim

> At a **matched realized token budget**, *where* a VLM spends visual tokens can dominate *how many*
> by **≥26×** — yet **no published crop/zoom policy beats simply spending the budget**, at either
> ~1500px or 4K. We give the quantity that decides it (**evidence-set coverage**), show by three
> internal interventions that the deficit **cannot be repaired inside the model**, trace the
> proposer's failure to a **raster-serialization artifact present in four architectures**, and
> deliver a **free gate** that makes allocation cost-effective in the regime where coverage is
> achievable.

---

## §1 — The comparison nobody makes

Pruning holds LLM-side tokens fixed; LLMind/Gizdov hold pixels; ViRGo holds seconds; crop/zoom
(V\*/SEAL, Zoom Eye, Chain-of-Spot, Visual CoT, DualFocus) hold **nothing** — they add passes, add
pixels, or both, and none reports what the same budget buys on a larger uniform image.

**Instrument.** Arms calibrated on the processor's own `image_grid_thw`; counts **measured, never
computed**; a **budget gate** voids contrasts spreading >10%. Dynamic range is architectural:
1944× (Qwen2-VL) → **1.5× (LLaVA-NeXT)**, reported as "NO AXIS", not a bound.

## §2 — ★★ THE FINDING: no policy beats the budget axis

Every policy charged for **every image token across every pass**, against the uniform sweep measured
**in-run on the same items**.

| policy | V\*Bench (n=191) | HR-Bench 4k (n=800, CircularEval) |
|---|---|---|
| grounding (Chain-of-Spot · Visual CoT · DualFocus) | **−6.2pp** @595 tok | +1.3pp n.s. @590 tok |
| **Zoom Eye (tree search)** | −5.2pp @2664 tok | **−15.3pp [−18.6,−11.8]** @2636 tok |
| ours, gated | **+5.4pp** @414 tok | −0.2pp @411 tok |

**Zoom Eye has the highest raw accuracy in both tables and still loses to one uniform image of its
own budget.** At 4K that loss is **−10.4pp [−14.1,−6.6] against uniform@1200 while spending 2.2× the
tokens** — significant at n=800. Per token it is the worst arm in either table (0.21 vs
uniform@300's 1.80).

The exchange rate that motivates all of this is real: oracle placement reaches **92.7%** against
uniform's **56.5%** at the same 300 tokens, and **no uniform rung catches it at 7957** — **≥26×**,
a lower bound set by the model's own ceiling, on four architectures.

⚠ These are **reimplementations of the published policies on a common backbone**, not released
checkpoints; V\*/SEAL's search module is absent. On V\*Bench no margin is individually significant
(n=191); at 4K Zoom Eye's loss is.

## §3 — Why: evidence-set coverage decides the sign

Two rules died on their own tests — **size** (HR-Bench is sub-token and allocation *lost*) and
**region count** (the oracle GT-box crop *wins* on relational questions). Both are causes of one
quantity.

| coverage of the evidence set | Δ vs uniform |
|---|---|
| **0% (window missed)** | **−15.6** [−26.0,−5.2] |
| **100%** | **+37.1** [+24.2,+50.0] |

Crossing zero at ~25%; **half of all windows miss**. Difficulty held fixed: a **70.8pp** gap in one
stratum — wrong-at-uniform items score 83.8% when covered and **13.0%** when missed, *below chance*.
Replicates on Qwen2-VL (−9.4 / +41.8pp). It also predicts a **competitor's** behaviour: grounding
fails on V\*Bench (median IoU **0.029**) and flips positive at 4K, because it must localize from a
300-token view that resolves 4032px targets and not 1500px ones.

## §4 — ★★ Causality: the deficit cannot be repaired inside the model

Three internal interventions, each **free if it worked**, each **one pass at identical tokens**:

| intervention | result |
|---|---|
| suppress the serialization sink | **null** (−0.5pp) = random/interior controls; −17.3pp on *prompt* tokens, so the machinery bites |
| amplify attention on the target | oracle-targeted **+5.8pp** = **16%** of the crop's +36.2pp; every practical arm = random |
| residual-stream steering | shared direction exists (cos 0.53–0.75); item's own displacement 47% reachable at L20; transferable vector **+0.0pp** |

**The oracle amplification set is 1 cell of 294.** The target is sub-token — there is nothing to
amplify. **Cropping creates tokens; attention and activations only reweight existing ones.** This
rules out "the model isn't looking in the right place": we made it look there and got a sixth of the
benefit. Allocation must add pixels — which is why every method in this literature acquires them.

⚠ *Scope, corrected 2026-09-15.* An earlier draft ended "...and why crops cost passes the budget
axis then outruns." That is true of the **crop-and-re-encode** family we test (Zoom Eye, ViCrop,
RAP, Chain-of-Spot), which pays a second full pass. It is **not** universal: **route-before-encode**
methods (SmartRes, 2608.01638) re-encode only selected patches and never pay a second full pass.
The claim is scoped to two-pass crop policies; see LITERATURE_GAPS.md third sweep.

## §5 — Interpretability: why the proposer misses

The sink is a **raster-serialization artifact**, not a corner effect — four architectures, two
families, two tokenization schemes. Last column **3.4–4.5×** where the row boundary is implicit; the
learned `image_newline` **2.0–2.3×** where it is explicit. Every CI excludes 1.0. **A newline
embedding is not a corner, not a pixel, not image content — only the serialization account predicts
it.** Transpose and interior-replay tests (1.1% vs 3.3% chance, *below*) rule out register tokens;
present at **L0**.

Read-outs **validated, not assumed**: the last-token read-out is best of seven (attention from the
noun *naming* the target is near chance — localization is not lexical lookup); the relative layer
block holds on Qwen2-VL (8/8 best layers inside; gt_pct 0.041 vs 0.390 outside).

## §6 — The constructive half: a free gate on *when* to spend

The two-pass gate detects coverage at **AUROC 0.835/0.885** — but by cropping first, which is the
expense. **`peak`, already computed by pass 1, predicts coverage at AUROC 0.788 for free.**

| | ungated | **gated** | tokens |
|---|---|---|---|
| V\*Bench margin vs bar | −2.5pp | **+5.4pp** [−1.4,+12.2] | −30% |
| HR-Bench 4k margin vs bar | −17.3pp | **−0.2pp** [−3.6,+3.3] | −30% |

**Gating is a large, free improvement at both scales** (+7.9pp / **+17.0pp** of margin) — and it
improves a **competitor**: Zoom Eye recovers **+5.7pp at 4K on 53% fewer tokens**. It is not
universal; it is matched to the attention proposer, because `peak` measures *that* proposer's
reliability.

⚠ **Stated honestly: the method's own positive margin is not statistically established.** Pooled in
its best regime — single-region questions at native resolution — it is **+3.9pp [−0.3,+8.2] at
n=515**. The lower bound is −0.3. This is reported, not buried.

## §7 — The regime where allocation pays (and v9's boundary, retracted)

v9 claimed a **scale** boundary: larger images → coarser localizer cells → worse proposals. A
controlled resolution ablation (same 800 rows at 1008/2016/4032px, bar measured at each scale)
**refutes it**: at **4× finer cells the margin is worse** (−3.6 / +0.1 / −0.2), and the budget-axis
term is nearly flat. **Proposal precision is not the binding constraint.**

Stratifying by category dissolves the boundary into §3 — HR-Bench is **50% multi-region**:

| scale | **cross** (multi-region) | **single** (one region) |
|---|---|---|
| 1008px | −4.4 | −2.4 |
| 2016px | −1.6 | **+1.6** |
| 4032px | −4.0 | **+3.5** |

The pooled −0.2pp at 4K is the average of +3.5 and −4.0.

⚠ *Priority, checked 2026-09-15.* **ViRGo (2606.21968) reported the trade-off first**, as the
"resolution–context trade-off": their Fig. 3a shows patch retrieval falling *below* the global
baseline as object size grows. §7 is therefore **not a novel boundary finding**. It is a novel
**explanation** of one — they bin by object *size*, have no region-count split, and their §7
Limitations names exactly our gap ("relational reasoning, compositional understanding, or
multi-object interactions — unexplored… the current routing signals do not yet fully characterize
the underlying perception difficulty"). Cite that sentence; present coverage as the answer to it.

> **Regime statement.** Allocation pays when **both** hold: the evidence is confined to **one
> region**, so one window can cover it; and there is **detail to reveal**, i.e. the evidence is
> sub-token in the *native* pixels. V\*Bench satisfies both. HR-Bench `cross` fails the first at
> every scale. Downsampled HR-Bench fails the second. One mechanism — coverage — not two.

*Caveat:* downsampling is not the same as a natively-smaller image, so this ablation varies available
detail as well as scale; it refutes the precision hypothesis but cannot test a pure scale hypothesis.

## §8 — Limitations and the negatives we keep

- **All three internal interventions negative** (§4) and **four attempts to win at 4K failed** — the
  causal backbone and the boundary, both reported rather than buried.
- Retracted: the AUROC-1.000 `obj` probe; the size boundary; the region-count boundary; the
  "interior optimum"; a mismatched capture fraction (83% → **88%**); "rows carry no excess mass";
  "ours is the only policy with a positive margin" (true at ~1500px, false at 4K); and **v9's
  scale-boundary mechanism**.
- **Three bugs in our own analysis, all caught and encoded as tests:** a bf16 checkpoint in fp16 gave
  all-NaN logits that read as a clean architecture-specificity negative; a row-segmentation error
  swallowed LLaVA's base image as "row 0"; and a pooled significance test computed the bar on all
  items while scoring an easier subset, turning **+3.9pp [−0.3,+8.2] into a spurious "significant"
  +5.2pp [+1.1,+9.4]**.
- **MMBench was rejected before use** — 512px images, no allocation headroom; a null there would
  have been a benchmark-selection artifact.
- Coverage is measured on **V\*Bench only** (n=191, ±7pp CIs) — HR-Bench ships no boxes.
- The multi-window intervention nets **zero** at the population level; sink n=24 images/model.

## §9 — Related work

On the **resource axis**: pruning holds LLM-side tokens fixed; LLMind/Gizdov hold pixels; ViRGo holds
seconds; crop/zoom hold nothing. **This work holds realized tokens fixed and moves them** — and
uniquely: scores the prior art against what its own budget buys uniformly, reports the regime where
allocation is *harmful*, gives the quantity that predicts which regime you are in, shows by
intervention that no internal alternative exists, and states where its own method stops paying.
